---
// /laws/ — v1.K tracker: listing-photo disclosure laws and bills (linkable asset).
// Rows come from src/data/laws.ts; every claim traces to docs/law-sources.md.
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";
import { LAWS, LAWS_CHECKED } from "../../data/laws";
import { PILLAR_URL } from "../../lib/links";

const path = "/laws/";
const title = "Listing Photo Disclosure Laws by State: 2026 Tracker";
const h1 = "Listing photo disclosure laws and bills, state by state";
const description =
  "Which states require disclosure of edited or AI-altered listing photos: California's sale and rental laws, plus the New York and DC bills, compared.";

const fmt = (iso: string) =>
  new Date(iso + "T12:00:00Z").toLocaleDateString("en-US", { year: "numeric", month: "long", day: "numeric", timeZone: "UTC" });

const faq = [
  {
    q: "Which states require disclosure of virtually staged or edited listing photos?",
    a: "As of the status check on this page, only California has an enacted law: AB 723 for sales, in force since January 1, 2026, and AB 2025 for rentals, which takes effect January 1, 2027. New York (A11635) and Washington, DC (B26-0858) have bills pending.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is there a federal law on altered listing photos?",
    a: "Not that we found. No federal bill on digitally altered real estate photos turned up when this tracker was last checked. Misleading advertising is regulated in general terms, but there is no photo-specific federal disclosure rule.",
  },
  {
    q: "Do these laws ban virtual staging?",
    a: "No. All four require disclosure, not a ban: label the altered image and give access to the original. Bans on specific edits come from MLS rules — CRMLS, for example, prohibits altering landscape or utility poles in listing photos.",
  },
  {
    q: "Do the laws cover rentals as well as sales?",
    a: "Increasingly, yes. California's original law covered sales only; AB 2025 adds rentals from 2027. Both pending bills, New York's and DC's, cover sale and lease from the start.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does AI-generated imagery count?",
    a: "Under California's rental law and New York's bill, explicitly. Civil Code § 1940.11 covers images fully generated by AI, and New York's A11635 adds a separate \"AI-generated image\" category that includes video. California's sales law and DC's bill define alteration by editing software or AI.",
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    "@type": "Article",
    headline: h1,
    description,
    mainEntityOfPage: `https://retouchlint.com${path}`,
    dateModified: LAWS_CHECKED,
    author: { "@type": "Organization", name: "RetouchLint" },
    publisher: { "@id": "https://retouchlint.com/#organization" },
  },
  {
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
      "@type": "Question",
      name: f.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: f.a },
    })),
  },
];

const related = [
  { url: "/laws/california-rentals/", anchor: "California rental listing photo law (AB 2025)" },
  { url: "/laws/new-york/", anchor: "New York's altered listing photo bill" },
  { url: "/laws/washington-dc/", anchor: "Washington, DC's listing photo bill" },
  { url: "/blog/california-real-estate-photo-disclosure/", anchor: "AB 723 explained" },
  { url: "/mls-photo-requirements/", anchor: "California MLS photo rules" },
  { url: "/tools/disclosure-generator/", anchor: "Disclosure statement generator" },
];
---
<ArticleLayout
  title={title}
  h1={h1}
  description={description}
  path={path}
  related={related}
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Home", url: "/" }, { name: "Disclosure laws", url: path }]}
  schema={schema}
>
  <p>
    California is the only state with a law requiring disclosure of digitally altered
    listing photos: AB 723 for homes for sale since January 1, 2026, and AB 2025 for rentals
    from January 1, 2027. New York and Washington, DC have bills modeled on it. All of them
    require the same two things, a label on or next to the altered image and access to the
    original, and they differ on who is covered, whether rentals count, and whether you must
    describe what changed.
  </p>
  <p class="text-sm">Status checked against official sources on {fmt(LAWS_CHECKED)}.</p>

  <h2>The tracker</h2>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Jurisdiction</th>
          <th>Law or bill</th>
          <th>Status</th>
          <th>Covers</th>
          <th>Applies to</th>
          <th>What it requires</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {LAWS.map((l) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{l.jurisdiction}</td>
            <td><a href={l.page}>{l.name}</a></td>
            <td><strong>{l.status}</strong>: {l.statusDetail}</td>
            <td>{l.covers}</td>
            <td>{l.who}</td>
            <td>{l.requires} (<a href={l.source}>official text</a>)</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What they have in common</h2>
  <p>
    All four start from California's definition. A photo is "digitally altered" when editing
    software or AI adds, removes or changes elements of the property: furniture, appliances,
    flooring, walls and, in California and DC, landscape, the façade and things outside the
    property such as utility poles and views. All four exclude routine adjustments: lighting,
    sharpening, white balance, color correction, angle, straightening, cropping and exposure.
    And all four make the original image available, either in the ad or through a link.
  </p>

  <h2>Where they differ</h2>
  <h3>Who is covered</h3>
  <p>
    AB 723 applies to real estate licensees and people acting for them. California's rental
    law applies to "a person", which on its face includes a landlord advertising their own unit. New
    York's bill names brokers, sellers, property owners and property managers. DC's bill
    works through its consumer-protection law, with a matching rule for licensees.
  </p>
  <h3>Describing the change</h3>
  <p>
    Only DC's bill requires a written description of the material differences between the
    altered image and the property's actual condition. Under the others, saying the image was
    altered is enough.
  </p>
  <h3>AI-generated images</h3>
  <p>
    California's rental law covers images fully generated by AI, and for those (and for
    renderings not taken with a camera) it drops the original-image requirement, since there
    is no original. New York's bill adds a separate "AI-generated image" category that
    includes video.
  </p>
  <h3>When the label is enough</h3>
  <p>
    DC's definition excludes adjustments that don't <em>materially</em> change the
    representation of the property, a slightly looser test than California's.
  </p>

  <h2>What to do if you work in more than one place</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Write one disclosure that meets the strictest rule you're subject to: a label on or next to the photo, a link to the original, and a short description of what changed. That covers DC's extra requirement too.</li>
    <li>Treat rentals the same as sales. California already does from 2027, and both pending bills do.</li>
    <li>Label fully AI-generated images, even where no original exists.</li>
    <li>Check your MLS separately. MLS rules can ban edits the law allows; see <a href="/mls-photo-requirements/">California MLS photo rules</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The <a href="/tools/disclosure-generator/">disclosure generator</a> writes the statement;
    the <a href={PILLAR_URL}>disclosure guide</a> covers keeping originals and records.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  {faq.map((f) => (
    <>
      <h3>{f.q}</h3>
      <p>{f.a}</p>
    </>
  ))}
  <p class="text-sm">
    Bills change as they move. Each row links to the official text; check it before relying
    on a pending bill's details. This is not legal advice.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
